<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonld={jsonld} navVariant="inner">
<section class="hero">
<div class="wrap">
<span class="eyebrow">Stage 2 — During production</span>
<h1>Eyes on your order, every week</h1>
<p class="lead">For programme orders and long runs, one inspection isn't oversight. Monitoring puts scheduled and unannounced visits through your entire production—with photo reports at every milestone.</p>
<div class="hero-ctas"><a class="btn btn-solid" href="/#book">Book an inspection</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/why-independent/">Why independent</a></div>
</div>
</section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>What production monitoring covers</h2></div></div><div class="grid"><a class="card rv" href="/#book"><h3>Milestone visits</h3><p>Material intake, machining, assembly, finishing, packing—photographed at each stage against the plan.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/clusters/jodhpur/"><h3>Unannounced checks</h3><p>The visits that keep subcontracting honest—possible only because we're minutes away, not a flight.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/#book"><h3>Schedule tracking</h3><p>Weekly real-progress reporting so a slipping vessel date is news in week 2, not week 8.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/during-production-inspection/"><h3>Early defect alerts</h3><p>Problems flagged to you the day they're seen, with the rework conversation held while it's cheap.</p></a></div></div></section><section><div class="wrap prose-wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>Who it is for, and what it costs</h2></div></div><p class="prose rv">Retail chains running repeat programmes, brands with strict spec tolerances, and any buyer whose order spans six weeks or more of production. Monitoring is priced from $199 per visit or as a weekly programme rate; most buyers pair it with a closing pre-shipment inspection so the final AQL check lands on a run that was never allowed to drift.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap prose-wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>Why one snapshot is not enough for a long run</h2></div></div><p class="prose rv">A single inspection is a photograph; a six-week production run is a film. On programme orders and large runs the risk does not sit at one moment — it moves. A line that started on-spec drifts as the pressure to hit the vessel date builds; a subcontractor is quietly brought in for the overflow; a material substitution three weeks in never reaches the pre-shipment sample. Monitoring replaces the single snapshot with scheduled milestone visits and unannounced checks across the whole window, each one photographed, so the order is watched the way you would watch it if you were standing in the cluster.</p><p class="prose rv">The unannounced visit is the part a fly-in inspector cannot offer. Because our inspectors are based in the clusters — minutes from the Jodhpur and Jaipur factories, not a flight away — a check can land on a day the factory did not plan for. That is what keeps subcontracting honest and progress claims accurate: the real answer to &ldquo;will it make the vessel?&rdquo; comes from a floor walked without notice, not a status email.</p></div></section><section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>Who runs a monitoring programme</h2></div></div><div class="grid"><a class="card rv" href="/buyers/retail-chains/"><h3>Retail chains with vendor programmes</h3><p>Repeat orders across a season need the same discipline on the fifth PO as the first. Monitoring gives your QA team a documented, photographed trail against your AQL and spec.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/buyers/brands/"><h3>Brands with tight spec tolerances</h3><p>When the finish, dimension or material has no margin, milestone checks catch drift before it becomes a container of near-misses.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/#book"><h3>Any order over six weeks in build</h3><p>The longer the run, the more room for a quiet substitution or an unbooked subcontractor. Weekly eyes close that window.</p></a></div></div></section><section><div class="wrap prose-wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>How monitoring is run and priced</h2></div></div><p class="prose rv">A programme is agreed up front: the milestones that matter for your product, the visit cadence, and the checklist and golden-sample references. Each visit produces a photographed milestone report within 24 hours; unannounced checks are scheduled at our discretion, not the factory&rsquo;s. Most buyers close the programme with a pre-shipment inspection so the final AQL check lands on the finished, packed goods. Pricing is from $199 per visit or as a weekly programme rate — buyer-paid and fixed at booking, never a percentage of your order and never paid by the factory.</p></div></section><section id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><div><h2>Production monitoring FAQ</h2></div></div>
    <div class="faq">
      <details class="rv"><summary>How is monitoring different from a single inspection?</summary><p>One inspection is a snapshot. Monitoring runs scheduled and unannounced visits through the entire production window, with photo reports at every milestone.</p></details>
      <details class="rv"><summary>What does production monitoring cost?</summary><p>From $199 per visit, or as a weekly programme rate.</p></details>
      <details class="rv"><summary>Who is production monitoring for?</summary><p>Retail chains running repeat programmes, brands with strict spec tolerances, and any buyer whose order spans six weeks or more of production.</p></details>
      <details class="rv"><summary>Are the factory visits announced?</summary><p>Both. Scheduled milestone visits are planned; unannounced checks are what keep subcontracting honest — possible because we are minutes away, not a flight.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
